# Biome 2, level 1 — The Long Valley
#
# The first board that does not fit on a phone. It opens showing all of itself, settles near the
# spring at the top, and is panned from there — which is the whole reason it is shaped the way it is.
#
# Sprawling rather than crowded. The footprint reaches five rows above and below the middle, but only
# twenty-eight cells are open: two chambers joined by a zigzag spine, with a corridor branching east.
# A dense board of the same footprint would be a worse puzzle and a far worse one to verify — the
# solvability gate searches placements, and open space is what makes that search explode.
#
# The spine is a corridor, so the water pair has exactly one route and it is ten conduits long. That
# is the point: the score curve makes a long route worth far more, and this is the first board with
# room for one. Every cell of it wants either a straight or the same 120-degree bend, so the puzzle is
# reading the corridor rather than hunting for a rare shape.

id: biome2-01
name: The Long Valley

base-score: 100

# The water spine alone clears this, and so does the wind branch plus most of the spine. Neither is
# forced, which is the choice PRD section 3.2A is about.
target-score: 900

tokens: 1
skips: 3

seed: 7

# The north chamber, around the water spring.
cell: 0,-5
cell: 1,-5
cell: -1,-4
cell: 0,-4
cell: 1,-4
cell: -1,-3
cell: 0,-3

# The spine, zigzagging due south. A straight line down is not a hex direction, so it alternates
# south-east and south-west and reads as a column.
cell: 0,-2
cell: -1,-1
cell: -1,0
cell: -2,1
cell: -2,2
cell: -3,3

# The east corridor, branching off the spine and turning south.
cell: 0,-1
cell: 1,-1
cell: 2,-1
cell: 3,-1
cell: 4,-1
cell: 4,0
cell: 4,1
cell: 4,2

# The south chamber, around the water hub.
cell: -3,4
cell: -2,3
cell: -1,3
cell: -2,4
cell: -1,4
cell: -3,5
cell: -2,5

spring: 0,-5 water
hub: -2,5 water

spring: 4,2 wind
hub: 2,-1 wind

# The spine needs two straights and eight of the same bend; the wind branch needs three straights and
# one bend. Everything beyond that is slack, because a corridor with no spare tile is a corridor that
# a single unlucky draw ends.
tile: 0,2 water x10
tile: 0,3 water x4
tile: 0,3 wind x4
tile: 0,2 wind x2

# The board's own solution, replayed by the solvability gate rather than searched for. The search cannot
# finish on a board this size — it exhausted six hundred thousand states in fourteen seconds without a
# verdict — so this is how a large level is certified. See AuthoredSolution for what that trade costs.
#
# Rotations are written down. They are optional in the format, because a plain corridor admits exactly
# one rotation per cell and the replay can find it — but this board has junctions, and at a junction
# more than one rotation joins something legally while only one continues the route. Leaving them out
# produced a replay that connected differently and then dead-ended six moves later.
#
# Four cells are not on either route: they absorb the surplus tiles the bag deals. None of the three
# skips is used, which is the slack a single unlucky draw needs. It reaches 1735 against a target of
# 900, so a player can leave the wind branch alone — which is the choice the target is set for.

solve: -2,4 rot 1
solve: 4,1 rot 1
solve: 1,-1 rot 0
solve: 0,-4 rot 1
solve: -3,4 rot 4
solve: -3,3 rot 5
solve: 4,0 rot 1
solve: -2,2 rot 2
solve: -2,1 rot 5
solve: -1,0 rot 2
solve: 0,-3 rot 1
solve: 1,-5 rot 0
solve: 0,-2 rot 2
solve: 4,-1 rot 1
solve: -1,-1 rot 5
solve: -1,-4 rot 3
solve: -1,4 rot 0
solve: 3,-1 rot 0
